# Machine-local Claude Code permissions. Personal to whoever is running it, and
# the tracked copy carried absolute paths under one developer's home directory
# plus references to internal/llm and internal/nn, removed long ago.
.claude/settings.local.json

# One-off browser scripts written against a particular machine's scratch
# directory. Useful while debugging, meaningless to anyone else.
frontend/scratch_test_*.mjs
issue-metadata-mismatch.md
node_modules
docs/assets/diagrams/generated/
docs/hooks/__pycache__/

# Anywhere else Python leaves bytecode. scripts/ has its own now that the
# benchmark tool lives there, and naming each directory as it appears is how
# one eventually gets committed.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
site/
# Stray data content (see data-readme.md)
data-old/
data/R scripts/
data/mbtiles/*.json
!data/mbtiles/style.json
pr.sh
pr-body.md
issue-*.md

# Environment files are never committed — anywhere, not just where one has
# already caught us out. Build-time configuration belongs to the deployment,
# and a tracked *.env also fails the repository's own secret-scanning gate.
# The .example files are documentation and stay tracked; they do not match
# these patterns.
*.env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!*.env.example

# Screenshots shared during development, not part of the source
screenshots-gitignored/

# Benchmark run history (scripts/dtbench.py). Machine-local by design: a
# measurement is only comparable against others taken on the same hardware, so
# a shared database would invite comparing a laptop against the server and
# reading the difference as a regression. It also rewrites on every run, which
# is the worst possible shape for a tracked binary.
benchmarks/dtbench.sqlite

# Generated benchmark reports. Rebuilt from the database on demand with
# `dt benchmark-report`, so the PDF is an output rather than a record — the
# runs table is the record, and that is already ignored above.
benchmarks/reports/
